<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Which Over-the-Toilet Storage Type Is Right for You?</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table is-style-stripes"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Best for</th><th>Drilling?</th><th>Hides clutter?</th><th>Budget</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Freestanding étagère (open shelves)</td><td>Renters, quick setup</td><td>No (anti-tip strap needs one small screw)</td><td>No</td><td>$$</td></tr><tr><td>Freestanding unit with a cabinet</td><td>Renters who want a tidy look</td><td>No (anti-tip strap needs one small screw)</td><td>Partly</td><td>$$</td></tr><tr><td>Floating shelves</td><td>Minimal, decorative look</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>$</td></tr><tr><td>Wall-mounted cabinet</td><td>Hiding bottles and backups</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>$$</td></tr><tr><td>Over-the-toilet medicine cabinet</td><td>Bathrooms with no other mirror storage</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>$$–$$$</td></tr><tr><td>Tank-top basket or tray</td><td>The smallest budget and space</td><td>No</td><td>Partly</td><td>$</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">$ = under about $25 · $$ = about $25–75 · $$$ = over $75. Prices vary by store and size.</figcaption></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Measure Before You Buy</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A common problem is a unit that doesn&#8217;t clear the toilet or blocks the tank lid. Take these four measurements first:</p>



<figure class="dsh-diagram" style="margin:2em 0;"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 820" width="100%" role="img" aria-labelledby="dsho-t dsho-d" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family:Poppins,'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;height:auto;display:block;border-radius:12px;"><title id="dsho-t">How to measure for over-the-toilet storage</title><desc id="dsho-d">Front view of a toilet with an over-the-toilet shelving unit, showing four measurements: A toilet width at its widest point, B height of the tank lid, C clearance between the lid and the first shelf, D wall width available.</desc><rect width="1200" height="820" fill="#F7F1E9"/><text x="60" y="88" font-size="44" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Measure These 4 Things First</text><text x="60" y="128" font-size="24" fill="#6E645C">before buying any over-the-toilet storage</text><text x="1140" y="80" font-size="20" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text><rect x="60" y="160" width="780" height="600" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D6CDC2" stroke-width="3"/><line x1="60" y1="720" x2="840" y2="720" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="4"/><g stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="6" fill="none"><line x1="230" y1="230" x2="230" y2="720"/><line x1="670" y1="230" x2="670" y2="720"/></g><g fill="#B0845C"><rect x="222" y="250" width="456" height="14" rx="6"/><rect x="222" y="345" width="456" height="14" rx="6"/></g><g><rect x="262" y="200" width="60" height="50" rx="8" fill="#8FA689"/><rect x="340" y="215" width="44" height="35" rx="6" fill="#ECCDA0"/><rect x="400" y="195" width="30" height="55" rx="6" fill="#C85A32"/><rect x="440" y="205" width="26" height="45" rx="6" fill="#342E2A"/><rect x="560" y="205" width="80" height="45" rx="8" fill="#ECCDA0"/><rect x="262" y="295" width="90" height="50" rx="8" fill="#C85A32"/><rect x="370" y="295" width="90" height="50" rx="8" fill="#8FA689"/><rect x="478" y="295" width="90" height="50" rx="8" fill="#ECCDA0"/></g><rect x="370" y="470" width="160" height="100" rx="10" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="6"/><rect x="350" y="570" width="200" height="54" rx="22" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="6"/><path d="M390 624 L510 624 L490 720 L410 720 Z" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#342E2A" stroke-width="6"/><g stroke="#C85A32" stroke-width="4" fill="#C85A32"><line x1="350" y1="655" x2="550" y2="655"/><line x1="350" y1="640" x2="350" y2="670"/><line x1="550" y1="640" x2="550" y2="670"/><line x1="720" y1="470" x2="720" y2="720"/><line x1="705" y1="470" x2="735" y2="470"/><line x1="705" y1="720" x2="735" y2="720"/><line x1="450" y1="359" x2="450" y2="470"/><line x1="435" y1="359" x2="465" y2="359"/><line x1="435" y1="470" x2="465" y2="470"/><line x1="100" y1="190" x2="800" y2="190"/><line x1="100" y1="175" x2="100" y2="205"/><line x1="800" y1="175" x2="800" y2="205"/></g><g fill="#C85A32"><circle cx="450" cy="655" r="20"/><circle cx="720" cy="595" r="20"/><circle cx="450" cy="415" r="20"/><circle cx="450" cy="190" r="20"/></g><g fill="#FFFFFF" font-size="22" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"><text x="450" y="663">A</text><text x="720" y="603">B</text><text x="450" y="423">C</text><text x="450" y="198">D</text></g><g font-size="22" fill="#342E2A"><text x="880" y="250" font-weight="700">A · Toilet width</text><text x="880" y="280" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">at its widest point; the unit&#8217;s</text><text x="880" y="302" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">inner leg width must be larger</text><text x="880" y="370" font-weight="700">B · Tank height</text><text x="880" y="400" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">floor to the top of the tank lid</text><text x="880" y="470" font-weight="700">C · Lid clearance</text><text x="880" y="500" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">first shelf must sit high enough</text><text x="880" y="522" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">to lift the tank lid off</text><text x="880" y="590" font-weight="700">D · Wall width</text><text x="880" y="620" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">space between vanity, walls</text><text x="880" y="642" font-size="17" fill="#6E645C">and towel bars</text></g></svg><figcaption style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;color:#6E645C;">The four measurements that decide which over-the-toilet storage fits.</figcaption></figure>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Toilet width (A):</strong> measure the widest part of the toilet, usually the tank or the bowl rim. A freestanding unit&#8217;s inner leg width must be larger.</li>



<li><strong>Tank height (B):</strong> measure from the floor to the top of the tank lid.</li>



<li><strong>Lid clearance (C):</strong> the lowest shelf must sit high enough above the tank that you can lift the lid off for repairs. Compare B with the height of the unit&#8217;s lowest shelf; a few extra inches of gap makes lifting the lid much easier.</li>



<li><strong>Wall width (D):</strong> check the space between the vanity, side walls, towel bars and light switches, so the unit or cabinet has room.</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Also check that nothing blocks the water supply valve behind the toilet. You should always be able to reach it quickly.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">No-Drill Over the Toilet Storage Ideas</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-02-etagere.jpg" alt="Freestanding white étagère above a toilet with folded towels, baskets and a plant" class="wp-image-899" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-02-etagere.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-02-etagere-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-02-etagere-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-02-etagere-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These options work in rentals or for anyone who doesn&#8217;t want to patch holes later.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. A Classic Freestanding Étagère</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A three-shelf étagère is the fastest way to add storage above the toilet. Look for adjustable feet so it stands level on uneven tile, and use an anti-tip strap (buy one if none is included). Freestanding units can tip if you pull on a top shelf.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. A Freestanding Unit with a Cabinet on Top</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If open shelves look messy to you, choose a unit with a closed cabinet at the top and one open shelf below. Hide mismatched bottles behind the doors and keep towels on display.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. A Space-Saver with Towel Bars</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some over-the-toilet units include a towel bar or hooks on the sides. In a bathroom with no room for a separate towel rack, this solves two problems with one piece.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. A Tank-Top Basket</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A low woven basket on the tank lid holds two or three spare rolls and a folded hand towel. Keep it light so you can lift it off when you need to open the tank.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. A Tank-Top Tray for Daily Items</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shallow tray corrals a flameless candle, a small plant and hand soap refills, so the tank lid stops collecting loose items. Keep it light and easy to lift off.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. A Leaning Ladder Shelf Beside the Toilet</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-05-ladder-shelf.jpg" alt="Leaning wooden ladder shelf beside a toilet with baskets of toilet paper, a hanging towel and a plant" class="wp-image-896" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-05-ladder-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-05-ladder-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-05-ladder-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-05-ladder-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the space directly above the toilet is blocked by a window or a mirror, a slim ladder shelf leaning beside it gives you vertical storage without drilling. Choose one with non-slip feet.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. A Slim Rolling Cart Next to the Toilet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Pair any over-the-toilet unit with a narrow rolling cart in the gap beside the toilet. It holds toilet paper and cleaning supplies at floor level, where you actually need them. See more ideas like this in our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bathroom-storage-ideas/">small bathroom storage ideas</a>, and learn how to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/under-bathroom-sink-organization-ideas/">organize under the bathroom sink</a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Wall-Mounted Over the Toilet Storage Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you can drill, wall-mounted storage looks more built-in and leaves the floor clear, which makes a small bathroom feel larger.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Two or Three Floating Shelves</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-04-floating-shelves.jpg" alt="Two white oak floating shelves above a toilet holding rolled towels, a flameless candle, a plant and small baskets" class="wp-image-898" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-04-floating-shelves.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-04-floating-shelves-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-04-floating-shelves-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-04-floating-shelves-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Floating shelves are the cleanest look (see our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-floating-shelves/">DIY floating shelves</a>). Keep them shallow so they don&#8217;t crowd you when you lean over the toilet to clean it, and mount them into wall studs or with anchors rated for the weight.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. A Wall Cabinet with Doors</h3>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-03-wall-cabinet.jpg" alt="Cream wall cabinet with glass doors above a toilet holding stacked towels and jars in a small bathroom" class="wp-image-900" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-03-wall-cabinet.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-03-wall-cabinet-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-03-wall-cabinet-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-03-wall-cabinet-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall cabinet above the toilet keeps everything behind doors; choose solid or frosted doors if you want to hide clutter completely. Choose one with an adjustable inner shelf so tall bottles and short jars both fit.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. An Over-the-Toilet Medicine Cabinet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If your vanity mirror has no storage behind it, a mirrored cabinet above the toilet adds a second mirror and hidden storage in one piece.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. A Cabinet Plus One Open Shelf</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mount a closed cabinet high for backup stock, then one open shelf below it for items you use daily. You get hidden storage and easy access in the same space.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. A Wall Grid or Pegboard</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small pegboard or wire grid lets you rearrange hooks, baskets and mini shelves whenever your needs change. It works especially well for brushes, clips and small hair accessories.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Wall-Mounted Towel Storage</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A wall rack with a shelf on top holds rolled towels and a row of hooks below, so towels move off the back of the door and the floor.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Style and Organize Over-the-Toilet Shelves</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Open shelves only look good if they&#8217;re organized. These tips keep them useful and tidy.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Use Matching Baskets</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Two or three matching baskets hide small items and make any shelf look intentional. Label the fronts so everyone in the household puts things back in the right place.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. Decant into Clear Jars</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Cotton balls, swabs and bath salts look tidy in clear glass jars with lids, and you can see at a glance when something is running low.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">16. Roll Towels Instead of Folding Them</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rolled towels often fit better on a shallow shelf than folded ones and are easier to grab without toppling the stack.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">17. Keep Heavy Items Low</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Store heavier items like extra toilet paper packs on the lowest shelf and light items at the top. This keeps freestanding units more stable.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">18. Add One Living Thing</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small plant that tolerates humidity softens a wall of storage and makes the bathroom feel less like a supply closet.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1.jpg" alt="Organized small bathroom with labeled baskets and floating shelves" class="wp-image-541" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-768x768.jpg 768w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-530x530.jpg 530w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-750x750.jpg 750w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-fully-organized-small-bathroom-shot-wide-showi-1-500x500.jpg 500w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What to Store Above the Toilet (and What Not To)</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Good to store here</th><th>Better somewhere else</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spare toilet paper</td><td>Heavy glass bottles on high shelves</td></tr><tr><td>Rolled towels and washcloths</td><td>Medications that need a cool, dry place</td></tr><tr><td>Backup soap, shampoo and refills</td><td>Anything you need every few minutes, like a toothbrush</td></tr><tr><td>Cotton pads, swabs and tissues in jars</td><td>Items that could fall into an open toilet</td></tr><tr><td>Baskets of hair accessories</td><td>Electrical tools with cords hanging near water</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-06-before-after.jpg" alt="Over the toilet storage before and after: rolls and bottles piled on the tank next to the same bathroom with shelves and baskets above the toilet" class="wp-image-897" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-06-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-06-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-06-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/over-toilet-06-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Skipping the anti-tip strap.</strong> Freestanding units are top-heavy once loaded.</li>



<li><strong>Blocking the tank lid or the water valve.</strong> You need access for repairs.</li>



<li><strong>Choosing deep shelves.</strong> Deep shelves crowd the space over the toilet and hide items at the back.</li>



<li><strong>Overfilling open shelves.</strong> Leave some empty space. It&#8217;s what makes open storage look calm.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Over the Toilet Storage FAQ</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is over-the-toilet storage safe?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It is safe when installed correctly. Anchor freestanding units to the wall with an anti-tip strap, mount wall cabinets into studs or with anchors rated for the load, and keep heavy items on lower shelves.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What can I put over my toilet if I rent?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A freestanding étagère or space-saver, a tank-top basket, and a leaning ladder shelf all add storage without drilling. Ask your landlord before using an anti-tip strap, since it needs one small screw, or look for adhesive options rated for the weight.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Should I choose open shelves or a cabinet?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Choose open shelves if you want a light, airy look and you&#8217;ll use baskets to keep things tidy. Choose a cabinet if you store lots of mismatched bottles and backups and want them out of sight.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The wall above the toilet is one of the easiest places to gain storage in a small bathroom. Measure the four points, choose the type that fits how you live, and keep the shelves organized with baskets and a little breathing room.</p>



